<SubSection title="Types of Charge" id="Types of Charge">
<p>The electron, proton, and neutron are fundamental particles that make up all atoms. Electrons and protons have an elementary charge equal in magnitude but opposite in sign. <b>Charge</b> is denoted by <i>q</i> and is expressed in Coulombs. One elementary charge equals <InlineEquation equation="1.602 x 10^{-19}C" />. </p>
 <p class="text-center"><em>Fundamental Particles</em></p>
  <DisplayTable id="dim-table">
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th>Fundamental Particle</th><th>Mass (g)</th><th>Elementary Charge</th><th>Charge (C)</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <th>Electron</th><td><InlineEquation equation="9.11 x 10^{-28}" /></td><td>-1</td><td><InlineEquation equation="-1.602 x 10^{-19}" /></td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <th>Proton</th><td><InlineEquation equation="1.67 x 10^{-24}" /></td><td>+1</td><td><InlineEquation equation="+1.602 x 10^{-19}" /></td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <th>Neutron</th><td><InlineEquation equation="1.67 x 10^{-24}" /></td><td>0</td><td>0</td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </DisplayTable>

<p> <b>Current</b> <i>i</i> or <i>q̇</i> is the flow or rate of charge expressed in Amperes or Coulombs per second. Charge can flow through conductive materials such as metals, ionic solutions, or ionized gases.</p>
<DisplayEquation title="" background="True" equation="i = \\dot{q} = \\frac{\\delta q}{\\delta t}" />
<p><b>Electrical energy</b> can describe two different types of energy: electrical potential energy and electromagnetic energy. <b>Electromagnetic energy</b> refers to electric and magnetic fields. <b>Electric potential energy</b> is the flow of electrical current which is relevant to conservation of charge and modeled through electrical circuits.</p> 
<p>An <b>electrical circuit</b> is a continuous loop where current moves from a power source, through components, and back to the source. Basic components in a circuit include a voltage source, resistors, capacitors, and inductors.</p>

 <p class="text-center"><em>Electrical Ciruit Components</em></p>
 <DisplayTable id="dim-table">
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th>Component</th><th>Function</th><th>Symbol</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <th>Voltage Source (Power Source/Battery)</th><td>Provides a fixed voltage across its terminals, pushing current through the circuit. <i>Expressed in volts (V)</i>.</td><td>				<Image src='/cons/VoltageSource.png' > </Image></td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <th>Capacitor</th><td>Stores energy in an electric field and resists sudden changes in voltage.<i>Expressed in Farads (F)</i>.</td><td><Image src='/cons/Capacitor.png' > </Image></td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <th>Resistor</th><td>Limits or controls the flow of current by converting some electrical energy into heat. <i> Expressed in Ohms (<InlineEquation equation="\\Omega"/>).</i></td><td><Image src='/cons/Resistor.png' > </Image></td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <th>Inductor</th><td>Stores energy in a magnetic field and resists sudden changes in current. <i> Expressed in henry (H).</i></td><td><Image src='/cons/Inductor.png' > </Image></td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </DisplayTable>
